# Source: Lecture Quiz 2 (solutions), third question.
id: factorial-wrong
title: A factorial loop with a bug
topic: Loops
level: 3
source: Lecture Quiz 2, factorial
tags: while, invariant, counterexample, product

task:
  The quiz asks to show that the triple below is correct, using the
  invariant $1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k)$, where
  $prod(k, 1, i, k) = 1 * 2 * ... * i$. The quiz calls the variable $f$
  "fact"; we rename it to avoid a clash with the factorial function.

  Check the invariant, and decide whether the triple is valid.

pre: i = 1 && f = 1 && n >= 1
post: f = prod(k, 1, n, k)

program:
  {P}
  {I}
  while (i < n) do {
    {I && i < n}
    {R2}
    f := f * i;      // b1
    {R1}
    i := i + 1       // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(i < n)}
  {Q}

verdict: invalid

step: Check the invariant of the quiz
rule: ASSIGN, SEQ, CONSEQUENCE
focus: b1, b2
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k))
  = 1 <= i + 1 <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k)
calc:
  wp(f := f * i, 1 <= i + 1 <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k))
  = 1 <= i + 1 <= n && f * i = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k)
vc: 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k) && i < n ==> 1 <= i + 1 <= n && f * i = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k)
status: fails
counterexample: i = 1, f = 1, n = 2
why:
  With $f = 1 * ... * i$, the body computes $f * i = 1 * ... * i * i$,
  while the invariant after the iteration needs $1 * ... * i * (i + 1)$.
  In the state $i = 1$, $f = 1$, $n = 2$: $f * i = 1$ but
  $prod(k, 1, 2, k) = 2$. The candidate is not preserved.

step: Find the real invariant
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  We trace the loop from $i = 1$, $f = 1$:

  | Loop head | $i$ | $f$ |
  |---|---|---|
  | before iteration 1 | $1$ | $1$ |
  | before iteration 2 | $2$ | $1$ |
  | before iteration 3 | $3$ | $2$ |
  | before iteration 4 | $4$ | $6$ |

  The body multiplies by the old value of $i$ before increasing it, so $f$
  is the product of the numbers below $i$: $f = 1 * 2 * ... * (i - 1)$.
set: I = 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k)
vc: i = 1 && f = 1 && n >= 1 ==> I
why:
  With $i = 1$ the product $prod(k, 1, 0, k)$ has no term, so it is $1$.
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, I)
  = 1 <= i + 1 <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i + 1 - 1, k)
set: R1 = 1 <= i + 1 <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i + 1 - 1, k)
calc:
  wp(f := f * i, R1)
  = 1 <= i + 1 <= n && f * i = prod(k, 1, i, k)
set: R2 = 1 <= i + 1 <= n && f * i = prod(k, 1, i, k)
vc: I && i < n ==> R2
why:
  $f * i = (1 * ... * (i - 1)) * i = prod(k, 1, i, k)$.

step: The postcondition at the exit
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
vc: I && !(i < n) ==> f = prod(k, 1, n, k)
status: fails
counterexample: i = 2, f = 1, n = 2
why:
  At the exit $i = n$, so the invariant gives $f = 1 * ... * (n - 1)$,
  which is $(n - 1)!$ and not $n!$.
vc: {i = 1 && f = 1 && n >= 1} while (i < n) do { f := f * i; i := i + 1 } {f = prod(k, 1, n, k)}
status: fails
counterexample: i = 1, f = 1, n = 2
result: The triple is not valid: the program computes $(n - 1)!$. For $n = 2$ it ends with $f = 1$ instead of $2$.

summary:
  The failed preservation check in the quiz was not a mistake in the proof
  but a symptom of a bug in the program. Swapping the two statements of
  the body, `i := i + 1; f := f * i`, gives a correct program, with
  invariant $f = prod(k, 1, i, k)$, as in the factorial exercise of the
  recipe.
